9287	Set up your site to respond appropriately to site errors, including 403 and 404 page responses.		11.1.6
9288	What are 403 and 404 responses?		11.1.6
9329	After selecting the desired formatters, click the settings gear in each row to change the settings for the formatter.		11.1.6
9361	@count contextual link@count contextual links		11.1.6
9289	When a user visits a web page, the web server sends a response code in addition to the page content. A normal, non-error response has code 200. If the page does not exist on the site, the response code is 404. If the page exists, but the user is not authorized to visit the page, the response code is 403. The core software provides default responses for both 403 and 404 codes, but if you prefer, you can create your own pages for each.		11.1.6
9290	What other errors can occur?		11.1.6
9291	Under some situations, your site can generate error messages. These can be due to user errors (such as entering invalid values in a form, or incorrect configuration), PHP runtime errors, or software bugs. Some errors may result in a <em>white screen of death</em> (a totally blank web page response); less drastic errors will generate error messages. You can configure what happens when an error message is generated.		11.1.6
9292	If desired, create pages to use for 403 and 404 responses. Note the URLs for these pages.		11.1.6
9293	In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Configuration</em> &gt; <em>System</em> &gt; <em>@information_link</em>.		11.1.6
9294	In the <em>Error pages</em> section, enter the URL for your 403/404 pages, starting after the site home page URL. For example, if your site URL is <em>https://example.com</em> and your 404 page is <em>https://example.com/not-found</em>, you would enter <em>/not-found</em>.		11.1.6
9295	Click <em>Save configuration</em>. You should see a message indicating that the settings were saved.		11.1.6
9296	In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Configuration</em> &gt; <em>Development</em> &gt; <em>@log_settings_link</em>.		11.1.6
9297	For a production site, select <em>None</em> under <em>Error messages to display</em>. For a site that is in development, select one of the other options, so that you are more aware of the errors the site is generating.		11.1.6
8577	Congratulations and welcome to the Drupal community.		11.1.6
8137	Drupal is an open source platform for building amazing digital experiences. It’s made, used, taught, documented, and marketed by the <a href="@drupal_community">Drupal community</a>. Our community is made up of people from around the world with a shared set of <a href="@drupal_values">values</a>, collaborating together in a respectful manner. As we like to say:		11.1.6
9298	<a href="@drupal_global_training_days">Global Training Days:</a> Helpful information for evaluating Drupal as a framework and as a career path. Taught in your local language.		11.1.6
9299	Get support and chat with the Drupal community on <a href="@drupal_slack">Slack</a> or <a href="@drupal_chat">DrupalChat</a>. When you’re looking for a solution to a problem, go to <a href="@drupal_support">Drupal Support</a> or <a href="@drupal_answers">Drupal Answers on Stack Exchange</a>.		11.1.6
